WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.301
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.301 --> 00:00:03.636
We're here with
Vice President Kamala Harris.

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:04.804
Obviously, you have to make
a great many sacrifices

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:06.105
to hold this office.

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:09.542
But is it true that you
can no longer send emojis?

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:12.145
[ Laughter ]

00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:14.614
-Okay, high-class problems.

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:16.783
[ Laughter ]

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:21.921
I have not received directly
an emoji in a year and a half.

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:25.191
-Wow. And you can't have your
staff, like, print them off?

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:26.759
-I mean, I suppose they could,

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:28.294
but they're not
coming to me directly.

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:31.998
Or, you know, for example,
our family is very competitive,

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:33.666
be it Wordle or other things.

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:38.037
And, you know, the family group
chat is no longer a thing.

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:40.239
-Wow.
-Like, you know, it was my

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:44.177
father-in-law's birthday,
and we were talking to the kids.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:45.544
And I thought, "Okay.
The kids can do

00:00:45.544 --> 00:00:47.547
a video for his birthday."
And, normally, I'd send

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:51.517
the family text, but this time,
we had to do like a phone tree.

00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:53.186
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:56.756
-It's very outdated to actually
do an old-school phone tree.

00:00:56.756 --> 00:01:00.226
-Yeah, yeah.
-I think one of the nice things

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:03.062
about the office is you get to
live in the Naval Observatory.

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:05.865
One of the current, I would say,
downsides of that

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:07.967
is the governor of Texas,
Greg Abbott,

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:11.370
is participating in
a piece of political theater.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:13.473
He is sending migrants,

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:15.675
basically dropping them off
at your doorstep.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:17.810
-Yeah.
-I don't think that, you know,

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.512
playing games
with people's lives

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.213
is the solution to this problem.

00:01:21.213 --> 00:01:24.284
But based on the record amount
of arrests at the border,

00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:25.985
it is a problem.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:28.120
I think we look to our leaders
at times like these.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.624
What is the path forward?

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:36.229
-Well, let's start with
the first point that you made.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:38.197
I mean, we're talking about
people who have

00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:41.000
fleed great harm --
They've fled great harm.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.537
And they are coming here
seeking refuge.

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:48.208
And talk about
political theater, I mean,

00:01:48.208 --> 00:01:50.276
playing games
with people's lives --

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.945
with their lives --
you know, there were mothers

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:55.981
with sleeping babies
getting off those buses.

00:01:55.981 --> 00:01:59.652
And I just think it's an
absolute dereliction of duty.

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:04.256
If you see a problem,
and if we agree that we need

00:02:04.256 --> 00:02:08.194
to address it,
then if you're a leader,

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.330
participate in a solution,
right?

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.334
When we first came in office,
the first bill

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:18.637
that we proposed was
for a pathway for citizenship --

00:02:18.637 --> 00:02:21.173
was to fix a broken
immigration system,

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:24.277
which was broken under
the previous administration.

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:25.811
Participate in the solution

00:02:25.811 --> 00:02:28.214
because we are
offering solutions.

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:30.917
But instead, this gamesmanship

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:35.088
with real human beings
who trust us...

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:38.391
-It's very haunting to watch

00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:42.928
human beings being used
as props like that.

00:02:42.928 --> 00:02:44.964
There's a great amount
of heaviness to your job.

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:47.667
Do you have any time
in your life to relax

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:50.202
and do anything
where you can unwind?

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:52.271
You mentioned Wordle,
but that doesn't take that long.

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:53.972
[ Laughter ]
You know what I mean?

00:02:53.972 --> 00:02:55.675
If you're spending more than
five minutes on Wordle,

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:57.776
I think it's time to...
-Well, no, I mean,

00:02:57.776 --> 00:03:00.079
to your point, there's also
"The New York Times" --

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:01.781
the crossword
you can do in seconds.

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:03.148
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
Oh, you do the mini?

00:03:03.148 --> 00:03:04.150
-Yeah, I do the mini.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.385
Don't tell people
you do the mini.

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:06.853
[ Laughter ]

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:10.056
I don't have time, right?
-That's right.

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:11.891
-I did it in 50 seconds.
I'm very proud of that.

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:13.693
-That's true.
If you can do it --

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:16.429
I don't want the Vice President
doing the Sunday crossword.

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:17.930
I don't know
what I'm talking about, yeah.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:22.535
-But we -- Doug and I,
one of our favorite shows

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.236
right now
is "House of the Dragon."

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:25.438
-Okay.
-We're watching that.

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:27.439
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:30.143
-Do you enjoy watching
a political system

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:31.644
that's maybe
more damaged than ours?

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:33.012
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:35.114
-Because there's
no ambiguity about it, right?

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.350
You're either riding a dragon
or you're not.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.986
[ Laughter ]

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:41.888
You know, we love --
And Ella's here.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:43.388
We love -- the whole family,

00:03:43.388 --> 00:03:46.325
we watch Formula 1
racecar driving.

00:03:46.325 --> 00:03:47.626
-All right.
Look at that.

00:03:47.626 --> 00:03:51.130
-So, like, Sunday mornings,
if we're all around --

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.899
depending -- I mean,
I was just in Japan yesterday,

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:56.336
but if it's close
to the same time zones...

00:03:56.336 --> 00:03:59.071
-That's nice.
When you and the first gentleman

00:03:59.071 --> 00:04:01.106
watch TV at night,
is one of you more likely

00:04:01.106 --> 00:04:02.308
to fall asleep first?

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.077
'Cause this is an issue
my wife and I have.

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:05.511
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:08.081
-Um, yeah, it depends.
-Okay.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:10.984
He can sometimes, you know,
start snoring.

00:04:10.984 --> 00:04:13.052
-Okay.
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:14.787
-Yeah.
-But, generally,

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:16.255
we're up at the same time.
-That's very nice.

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.324
That's good.
You know, again,

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:19.892
I think it can get overlooked.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.194
And I'm sure,
over the course of your day,

00:04:21.194 --> 00:04:23.062
you have to focus on the job
the way anyone

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:25.865
focused on the job in the past.
But you did make history.

00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:29.435
And I'm wondering, are there
times when it sinks in for you

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.170
maybe more than others
that you are the first woman

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.339
to hold this office?

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:36.542
This is a photo that I just
think speaks to the fact that,

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:38.678
for these kids there,
that must be a thing that's,

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:40.446
I mean, hard to fathom.

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.282
-You know, that little girl
that you're showing here,

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:44.917
she was so spectacular.

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:49.222
This was an elementary school
in Connecticut.

00:04:49.222 --> 00:04:53.693
And I said to her,

00:04:53.693 --> 00:04:57.330
"You know, you can be anything
you want to be."

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.766
And this kid looked back at me,
in my eyes, and she said,

00:05:00.766 --> 00:05:02.568
"Well, I intend to be
everything."

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:04.537
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:07.240
-There you go.
-Right?

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.842
It was so --
I mean, you know,

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:11.877
those are moments of pure joy,
right?

00:05:11.877 --> 00:05:20.152
When you see that kind of
light and enthusiasm, that --

00:05:20.152 --> 00:05:22.722
-You must never --
when you're in a class

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:25.324
with elementary school students,
you must not want to leave

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:27.693
because I imagine the rest of
your day is pretty downhill.

00:05:27.693 --> 00:05:29.094
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.094 --> 00:05:34.100
-But, I mean, truly, you know,
when you see our kids --

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.368
and I truly believe
that they are --

00:05:36.368 --> 00:05:38.404
our children, they are the
children of our country,

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:40.273
of our communities.

00:05:40.273 --> 00:05:45.777
I mean, our future is really
bright if we prioritize them.

00:05:45.777 --> 00:05:49.115
And, therefore,
prioritize the climate crisis

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:50.682
and the need to address it.

00:05:50.682 --> 00:05:52.451
If we think about our children,

00:05:52.451 --> 00:05:55.787
prioritizing the need
to bring our country together.

00:05:55.787 --> 00:05:59.191
I travel around the world.
I've met 100 world leaders

00:05:59.191 --> 00:06:02.161
either by phone or in person,
talked with them, right?

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:03.963
From prime ministers
to presidents

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:06.132
to kings to chancellors.

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:08.934
And we still,
as the United States,

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:11.037
can walk in these rooms

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:13.239
with a certain level
of authority

00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:16.041
to talk about
the importance of democracy,

00:06:16.041 --> 00:06:19.946
the importance of rule of law,
the importance of human rights.

00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:23.382
And that gives us
standing in the world.

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:25.817
And when we continue
to invest in our country

00:06:25.817 --> 00:06:28.120
and invest in those priorities,

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.523
I think it's for the betterment
of everyone.

00:06:30.523 --> 00:06:32.625
But certainly, when we think
about it in terms of

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:33.826
the future of our children,

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:35.561
I think we have
a great responsibility.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:38.464
-You --
[ Applause ]

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:42.501
Obviously not a lot of people
have had your job,

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:45.404
but you do work
with someone who has.

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:46.605
You work with
a former vice president.

00:06:46.605 --> 00:06:48.073
-Yes, yes, our president.

00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:52.478
-Does the president ever also
give advice on your position?

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.780
-You know, I will tell you,

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.149
first of all,
I loved Joe Biden.

00:06:57.149 --> 00:06:58.918
And -- I really do.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:01.253
-It's probably for the best
that he can't send emojis,

00:07:01.253 --> 00:07:02.922
let's just say.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:05.691
-But, you know,
he is very thoughtful.

00:07:05.691 --> 00:07:07.292
And he is very kind.

00:07:07.292 --> 00:07:09.896
And, you know,
so he is thoughtful about,

00:07:09.896 --> 00:07:12.465
you know, the American people,
in terms of the work we've done.

00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:14.466
And it's been historic work,
how we've lifted up

00:07:14.466 --> 00:07:17.903
working families, how we've
invested in infrastructure.

00:07:17.903 --> 00:07:20.973
The biggest investment
in the climate crisis,

00:07:20.973 --> 00:07:23.042
historic, $370 billion.

00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:25.444
But what I will say, in terms of
the interpersonal relationship,

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:31.284
is he knows the job, and he
cares about the partnership.

00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:36.222
And in that way,
it makes it a real joy,

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:39.058
given all that
we actually need to address,

00:07:39.058 --> 00:07:40.892
in terms of the challenges
our country faces.

00:07:40.892 --> 00:07:42.528
But he really is
quite wonderful.

00:07:42.528 --> 00:07:44.296
And, yes,
having been vice president,

00:07:44.296 --> 00:07:46.098
he knows what the job involves.

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:49.435
-Well, I appreciate
that he and you both

00:07:49.435 --> 00:07:51.003
made time for you
to be here today.

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:52.371
It was really special
talking to you.

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:54.840
-Thank you.
-And do come back soon.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.676
You guys, that's our
vice president, Kamala Harris.

00:07:57.676 --> 00:07:58.877
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:58.877 --> 00:08:01.180
We'll be right back
with Geena Davis.

